WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.393
ALICE starts where a stylish runner concept meets the cold math of a real table. The fantasy matters.

2
00:00:06.393 --> 00:00:12.786
The look, the attitude, the role in the crew all need to survive contact with initiative, recoil, drain, money, availability, and the kind of mission that punishes vague confidence.

3
00:00:12.786 --> 00:00:19.179
First, she reads the sheet like a professional looking for pressure points. What is the runner actually good at.

4
00:00:19.179 --> 00:00:25.571
Where does the build fold. Which weakness is deliberate, and which one only appeared because the numbers were hard to see.

5
00:00:25.571 --> 00:00:31.964
Then she turns tradeoffs into plain language. Chrome buys speed and costs Essence. Magic opens doors and demands discipline.

6
00:00:31.964 --> 00:00:38.357
Gear solves problems until legality, concealment, noise, and cash start pushing back.

7
00:00:38.357 --> 00:00:44.750
A clever trick is not useful if the table cannot understand when it works.

8
00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:51.143
When the video moves from portrait to choices to warnings, the voice keeps one continuous line of thought: not scene labels, but momentum.

9
00:00:51.143 --> 00:00:57.536
We are watching a concept become table-ready, a first draft becoming something the whole crew can trust when the run starts moving.

10
00:00:57.536 --> 00:01:03.929
ALICE does not take authorship away from the player.

11
00:01:03.929 --> 00:01:10.321
She keeps the concept intact while making consequences visible early enough to choose them on purpose. The GM gets clearer risk.

12
00:01:10.321 --> 00:01:16.714
The player gets a runner who can enter the scene with fewer surprises and better reasons.

13
00:01:16.714 --> 00:01:23.107
The point is not optimization for its own sake. It is confidence.

14
00:01:23.107 --> 00:01:29.500
A character who still feels like the person you imagined, but now has a structure strong enough for the shadows to test.
